CPU baseline for mlir-aie ml/resnet/layers_conv2_x -- Desktop 2 (Phoenix, Ryzen 7 8700G)
2026-09-07. All five rows captured in ONE sitting, per the NPU-drift invariant.

WHY: layers_conv2_x -- three ResNet bottleneck blocks chained core-to-core across three
columns, int8, ObjectFifo to ObjectFifo, ONE dispatch for the whole chain -- has run and
PASSed on this machine since 2026-09-06 (mlir_aie_magika_mobilenet_npu.log:18, 1888.5us),
and nobody had ever measured what a CPU does the same work in. It was the single most
promising remaining target: unlike attention_bf16 it is big enough to amortize the
measured dispatch floor (dispatch_floor_npu.log) AND it is built on mlir-aie's own
validated int8 conv kernels rather than a hand-rolled inner loop. It is also the only
design on this hardware matching the repo's own XINT8 thesis.

The two previous kernels both lost after being built. This one was measured first.

WORKLOAD (from the design's own torch reference, test.py): input 1x64x32x32, stride 1,
3x3 padded, spatial stays 32x32 throughout.
  shortcut  64 -> 256  1x1                                              33.55 MFLOP
  block 0:  64 -> 64 1x1 | 64 -> 64 3x3 | 64 -> 256 1x1  (+skip)       117.44
  block 1: 256 -> 64 1x1 | 64 -> 64 3x3 | 64 -> 256 1x1  (+skip)       142.61
  block 2: 256 -> 64 1x1 | 64 -> 64 3x3 | 64 -> 256 1x1  (+skip)       142.61
  TOTAL                                                                436.21 MFLOP

=== MEASURED ===
                                          time        throughput
  NPU int8, hardware bracket            1869.6 us      233 GOPS
  NPU int8, END-TO-END (wall)           2497.8 us      175 GOPS
  CPU torch fp32 (8 threads)            1856   us      235 GFLOPS
  CPU ORT CPU EP, fp32                   815   us      535 GFLOPS
  CPU ORT CPU EP, QDQ int8 (VNNI)        295   us     1481 GOPS

NPU rows: `python3 test.py` in the mlir-aie ironenv; the design's own harness prints both
brackets. PASS (numerics verified by the design itself).
CPU rows: kernels/conv2x_baseline/cpu_baseline.py. torch cross-checked in two envs
(ironenv 1.857ms, resnet_env 1.856ms). int8 is ORT's own static QDQ quantizer with random
calibration -- LATENCY ONLY, never used for an accuracy claim; the QDQ graph shape and the
int8 kernels ORT selects do not depend on the scale values.
VERIFIED that the int8 row is really int8, since the whole 6.3x rests on it: after ORT's
own graph optimization the executed graph is
  {QLinearConv: 10, QLinearAdd: 3, QuantizeLinear: 1, DequantizeLinear: 1, Transpose: 2}
i.e. all 10 convs and all 3 skip-adds run as integer kernels, with a single quantize at the
input and a single dequantize at the output -- not an fp32 graph carrying stray Q/DQ pairs.
ONNX artifacts live in scratch/conv2x/ (git-ignored, not tracked). Regenerate with
cpu_baseline.py --mode torch --export ... then --mode quant.

=== THE ANSWER: THE CHAINED INT8 DESIGN LOSES, AND NOT NARROWLY ===
Like for like -- int8 on NPU against int8 on CPU:
    CPU is 6.3x faster than the NPU's hardware bracket (1869.6 / 295)
    CPU is 8.5x faster than the NPU end to end  (2497.8 / 295)
The NPU needs 436 MFLOP to take 2.5 ms; the same 8-core Zen4 that hosts it does the work
in 0.295 ms using VNNI.

This is the THIRD consecutive negative result on hand-written kernel / subgraph
acceleration (GroupNorm splice, attention kernel, now this) -- counting only that category;
the MobileNetV2 and stock-MobileViT losses are VitisAI EP results, a different thing. It is
the first of the three that cost an afternoon of measurement instead of weeks of kernel
writing, which is the whole point of having measured the floor first.

=== WHY THE CPU BASELINE CHOICE ALMOST INVERTED THE CONCLUSION ===
torch fp32 is 1856us. The NPU hardware bracket is 1869.6us. Had this been benchmarked
against torch alone -- the baseline kernels/attention_bf16 used -- the honest-looking
headline would have been "the chained int8 design reaches parity with CPU", off by a
factor of 6.3 from the like-for-like answer. ORT's CPU EP is 2.3x faster than torch on the
identical fp32 graph, and its int8 path is another 2.8x on top.
This is the same lesson the MobileViT splice produced (torch vs numpy attention, 9x,
splice_wall_clock_npu.log) arriving from the opposite direction, and it is now two for two:
ON THIS PROJECT, THE CPU KERNEL CHOICE HAS DECIDED THE VERDICT MORE OFTEN THAN THE NPU HAS.
Any future NPU-vs-CPU claim here must name which CPU implementation it beat.

=== INDEPENDENT CORROBORATION OF THE DISPATCH FLOOR ===
This design's harness reports both brackets, so it cross-checks dispatch_floor_npu.log
(measured the same day on a no-compute passthrough, a completely different design):
    end-to-end 2497.8 - hardware 1869.6 = 628.2 us of host-side cost
    passthrough floor measured separately: 617.0 us wall, of which 447.3 us host
Two unrelated designs -- a no-compute passthrough and a 3-block CNN with real weights --
agree on the per-call host cost to within about 2%. That is the stronger result here: this
morning's floor was explicitly caveated as "one design, one data point, a floor not a
universal constant", and it now reproduces on an independent design. The slightly higher
628us is consistent with this design carrying more buffers (activations plus three blocks'
weights) than the passthrough's three. The floor is not an artifact of the passthrough.

=== SCOPE: WHAT THIS CLOSES, AND WHAT IT EXPLICITLY DOES NOT ===
CLOSED -- THIS DESIGN AT THIS SHAPE. layers_conv2_x at 32x32x64, 436 MFLOP, 3 columns,
loses to Zen4+VNNI by 6.3-8.5x. That matters because it fixed every flaw diagnosed in
attention_bf16 (right dtype, right MAC path, dispatch amortized to ~25% of wall, no
two-process handoff) and still lost, so none of those were the binding constraint.

OPEN -- AND DELIBERATELY NOT CLOSED: whether the OP CLASS loses. Do not read this log as
"int8 chained conv is dead"; it is one shape and one column count, and the gap to the same
silicon's own demonstrated ceiling is unexplained. The 4-column bf16 matmul reached
895 GFLOPS here (mlir_aie_bf16_matmul_npu.log) against this design's 233 GOPS -- ~4x -- so
the array does far more than this design extracts. Two untested candidates for that 4x,
neither of which this run distinguishes:
  - COLUMN COUNT. layers_conv2_x uses 3 columns; the 895 GFLOPS run used 4. Part of the
    gap, but 3-vs-4 cannot be all of it.
  - SPATIAL SIZE / TILE UTILIZATION. 32x32 is small: short rows, small tiles, little work
    per DMA transfer -- structurally the same problem that sank attention, which would mean
    the conv kernels are underutilizing the MAC array at this shape rather than at all
    shapes. `ml/bottleneck` runs standalone on this chip; running it at 32x32 against a
    larger spatial and watching whether GOPS scales is the experiment that would turn
    "this design at this shape loses" into either "this op class loses" or "this op class
    needs spatial >= N".
Also untested: whether xrt::runlist batching (verified present in this XRT install and
bound in pyxrt) recovers any of the 628us of host cost. It would not close a 6.3x gap, so
it is no longer on the critical path for this question.
